WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.020 --> 00:00:03.400
Let's cover some theory - the Kuleshov effect.

00:00:09.400 --> 00:00:10.990
A quick bit of vocabulary:

00:00:10.990 --> 00:00:15.180
Throughout this when we're bringing up examples, we're going to be talking about "The Editor"

00:00:15.180 --> 00:00:18.060
and we're going to be using this title in the abstract,

00:00:18.080 --> 00:00:20.620
in the same way that we do when we're discussing "The Author."

00:00:20.620 --> 00:00:24.480
We're not necessarily talking about the individual who does the editing

00:00:24.480 --> 00:00:28.720
as much as we're talking about the concept of the person who does the editing.

00:00:28.720 --> 00:00:32.520
Because in reality it might be several people or it might be one.

00:00:32.520 --> 00:00:34.940
(And here's a link if you want to know more about that.)

00:00:34.940 --> 00:00:38.740
Also our examples are going to be taken mostly from "10 Cloverfield Lane"

00:00:38.740 --> 00:00:42.200
a 2016 film with pretty solid editing all around.

00:00:42.460 --> 00:00:46.320
"It is stumbling around isn't helping anything. What you heard earlier?

00:00:46.600 --> 00:00:52.940
"Was him knocking over a shelf with a whole week's worth of food which he's sorry for, correct?"

00:00:55.540 --> 00:00:56.340
"Totally."

00:01:01.080 --> 00:01:02.200
"Let's go."

00:01:02.200 --> 00:01:06.180
So, Lev Kuleshov was a Soviet filmmaker in the early 20th century

00:01:06.180 --> 00:01:09.060
But really he was a theoretician.

00:01:09.060 --> 00:01:13.820
He made movies but he was more interested in the ideas of how movies worked,

00:01:13.840 --> 00:01:16.780
in particular, the psychological mechanics of editing.

00:01:16.780 --> 00:01:19.920
Now if you go and read up on this you're going to see the word "montage" a lot,

00:01:19.920 --> 00:01:22.800
So I think this is a good moment to maybe clear this up.

00:01:22.800 --> 00:01:26.360
The thing to keep in mind is that this was literally a century ago.

00:01:26.360 --> 00:01:30.920
The roots of Modern film theory largely originated in France in the 1910's

00:01:30.920 --> 00:01:35.640
so the first waves of film theory in America, England, Germany and the Soviet Union,

00:01:35.660 --> 00:01:37.900
heavily borrow the French vocabulary.

00:01:37.900 --> 00:01:41.340
French writers differentiated editing into two concepts:

00:01:41.340 --> 00:01:45.740
'Couper' or cutting, the mechanical process of putting the film together,

00:01:45.740 --> 00:01:51.280
and 'Monter', the creative act of editing of which the end product is montage.

00:01:51.300 --> 00:01:57.160
So while modern English theory uses the word "montage" to describe a specific kind of sequence

00:01:57.160 --> 00:02:01.720
where a series of mostly discontinuous clips are shown in fairly rapid succession,

00:02:01.720 --> 00:02:07.280
the early use of the word montage refers to what we would today simply consider editing.

00:02:07.280 --> 00:02:10.600
This is important to keep in mind because if you're reading old theory,

00:02:10.640 --> 00:02:15.600
it's easy to trick yourself into thinking the theories only apply to what we call a montage,

00:02:15.600 --> 00:02:19.080
But really these ideas apply to all editing.

00:02:19.080 --> 00:02:24.860
So in the early 1920's, Kuleshov is teaching at the Soviet National Film School, and he's telling his students,

00:02:24.860 --> 00:02:31.940
he's writing, that the soul of cinema is in the edit - it's in the way that two shots interact.

00:02:31.940 --> 00:02:37.120
In fact he argues that acting itself can be completely suborned by editing.

00:02:37.120 --> 00:02:40.440
It is not the actor that matters, it is the cut.

00:02:40.440 --> 00:02:46.240
"More meaning", he says, "is created by the interaction of two shots than by any shot in isolation."

00:02:46.240 --> 00:02:49.900
To demonstrate this he puts together a famous editing exercise.

00:02:49.900 --> 00:02:52.980
An actor sits and stares at the camera with a blank expression.

00:02:52.980 --> 00:02:58.900
This shot is then intercut with a number of other images: war footage; a hot meal; children playing; and so on.

00:02:58.900 --> 00:03:03.920
The perception on the part of the audience is that the face takes on the implications of reaction.

00:03:03.920 --> 00:03:09.680
The neutral expression looking sad or hungry or worried in turn following the war, food, and child.

00:03:09.800 --> 00:03:15.960
The meaning is created not from either the A shot, or the B shot, it is created from the juxtaposition of the two,

00:03:15.960 --> 00:03:18.920
because there's a couple of things going on here.

00:03:18.920 --> 00:03:20.450
First is the idea of eyeline.

00:03:20.450 --> 00:03:23.660
This is another thing that we address briefly in Suicide Squad,

00:03:23.660 --> 00:03:29.320
the instinct to follow someone's eye line, that if someone on screen is looking somewhere and we cut away,

00:03:29.320 --> 00:03:33.240
it is assumed that the B shot is the thing that they are looking at,

00:03:33.240 --> 00:03:37.400
even if A and B aren't factually anywhere near each other.

00:03:37.400 --> 00:03:42.660
A character looks somewhere in the A shot, we assume the B shot is what they were looking at.

00:03:42.920 --> 00:03:48.000
The second is the idea of meaning, or what it means when we *say* "meaning".

00:03:48.000 --> 00:03:51.740
The instinct is to assume that it's a deeper, metaphorical kind of meaning,

00:03:51.740 --> 00:03:56.000
that we're saying theme and value are created by the interaction of two shots.

00:03:56.000 --> 00:04:03.240
And while that is true - metaphor is created by the interaction between shots - it's also a lot more mundane.

00:04:03.240 --> 00:04:06.140
It's the kind of thing you're not even thinking about.

00:04:06.140 --> 00:04:08.060
Let's look at that Cloverfield shot again.

00:04:08.060 --> 00:04:12.880
The meaning that's created is that these two shots are in the same space.

00:04:12.880 --> 00:04:16.940
The meaning is a better understanding of the geography of that space.

00:04:16.940 --> 00:04:20.040
The meaning is "This is what Michele is seeing."

00:04:20.040 --> 00:04:23.180
The very next cut is to a close-up of the slaughtered pigs.

00:04:23.180 --> 00:04:27.160
Even though this is not contiguous with Michele's actual field of vision-

00:04:27.160 --> 00:04:31.780
it is not a literal point of view shot because human eyes don't have a zoom function-

00:04:31.880 --> 00:04:38.540
it is emotionally contiguous. By cutting from far to close, the editor is saying that this is important.

00:04:38.540 --> 00:04:44.680
They are highlighting the emotional impact- the sense that a shocking thing seems to consume our focus.

00:04:44.680 --> 00:04:50.520
Then, Kuleshov Effect in full force, the Editor cuts back to Michele for her reaction.

00:04:50.520 --> 00:04:52.500
A shot. B shot.

00:04:52.660 --> 00:04:58.420
The simple fact that we are even able to describe this as a "reaction shot" is owed to this psychological

00:04:58.420 --> 00:05:05.580
phenomenon. This forms the logic behind the rules and dialect of film editing: the shot/reverse-shot of dialogue,

00:05:05.580 --> 00:05:11.420
the progression from establishing shots inward to close-ups, the preservation of screen direction through travel,

00:05:11.420 --> 00:05:12.380
and so on.

00:05:12.600 --> 00:05:17.680
Because this meaning becomes inherent to the sequence, a careful consideration of placement, order,

00:05:17.680 --> 00:05:23.620
and timing is necessary to both create desired meaning and to avoid undesired meaning.

00:05:23.620 --> 00:05:30.780
Now, what do I mean by "undesired meaning"? Well, mistakes mostly. Confusion is a kind of meaning,

00:05:30.780 --> 00:05:37.240
in the same way that incomprehensible noise is still an audio signal. This is actually an important paradigm

00:05:37.240 --> 00:05:44.280
shift in the way that you look at editing. A bad edit- Suicide Squad- isn't generally bad because of some

00:05:44.280 --> 00:05:50.660
inherent mechanical failure, it's bad because it creates undesired meaning. It creates confusion

00:05:50.660 --> 00:05:56.560
when confusion wasn't intended. This car crash is confusing because it's supposed to be confusing,

00:05:56.560 --> 00:06:02.840
you're supposed to get disoriented. It's emotionally and narratively appropriate, thus the confusion becomes

00:06:02.840 --> 00:06:05.500
an aesthetic choice rather than a mistake.

00:06:05.780 --> 00:06:10.699
Now, one of the things that I do need to bring up. I've recreated Kuleshov's demonstration here

00:06:10.700 --> 00:06:15.780
but you're probably thinking to yourself that it doesn't work nearly as well as I'm insisting that it does,

00:06:15.840 --> 00:06:21.520
and there's a couple reasons for that. First is that you as a viewer have grown up your entire life watching movies

00:06:21.520 --> 00:06:27.320
and television. The language of film, the language of continuity editing, is very natural to you.

00:06:27.320 --> 00:06:33.700
You are used to quickly picking up on the visual cues of environment, so discontinuous location is more likely

00:06:33.700 --> 00:06:39.240
to trip you up. Second is resolution. I'm showing you these images in full-color high-definition

00:06:39.240 --> 00:06:45.520
where Kuleshov's original experiment would have looked a lot more like this: [SFX old film reel rolling throughout]

00:06:55.080 --> 00:07:01.160
This lower resolution is important because it taps into another related idea, which is closure. Now, this is

00:07:01.160 --> 00:07:06.280
Now, this is a subject all its own, but the core is that the more iconic or cartooned something is,

00:07:06.280 --> 00:07:10.380
the easier it is to accept discontinuities or unrealities.

00:07:10.380 --> 00:07:15.760
Now, to wrap this up, I do want to throw in some caveats. The edit is the final interpretive stage in

00:07:15.760 --> 00:07:22.340
creating a movie, in building its meaning, before it is handed over to the audience; and, owing to that position,

00:07:22.340 --> 00:07:29.420
it is so powerful in its ability to reshape meaning that all the prior stages- writing *and* performance-

00:07:29.420 --> 00:07:35.940
reposition themselves to accommodate. Things are written and performed with an eye towards the edit.

00:07:35.940 --> 00:07:42.140
Kuleshov was being purposefully dramatic about the all-consuming power of the edit in creating the meaning of film,

00:07:42.140 --> 00:07:46.440
the ability to erase the actor, but only a little bit.

00:07:46.440 --> 00:07:53.300
This quirk of our brains, this perception of continuity and relation between two disconnected images, is

00:07:53.300 --> 00:08:00.680
second only to the perception of motion itself in how fundamental it is to the very structure of cinema.

00:08:01.060 --> 00:08:10.680
[SFX cheerful outro music]

